requirements.notes:
Body interruption points proven at BODY_VERIFIED, with real SIGKILL under supervisord:
- while waiting at an APPROVAL boundary: body_supervised, product_path, cortex_rehearsal, the 3 chromebook kills, and vepmc;
- after a founder detach command (cortex_rehearsal, second kill);
- SIGSTOP, then a torn ledger append, then SIGKILL, ending in tail quarantine (product_path);
- during learning and revert (improvement_attacks, forked Body);
- presence and heartbeat after a kill.

At UNIT_VERIFIED: the ledger tail tests, and the cooperative kill_at_step hook in DurableWorkflow, loom and foundry workflows (an in-process flag, not a real signal).

All Body kills land before dispatch of a consequential action. Not interrupted anywhere:
- the Body between grant_dispatch and receipt of a non-retry-safe action;
- the separate-process appraiser in the middle of an appraisal;
- the console or remote HTTP service in the middle of a request (they are only closed in an orderly way);
- the Capability Genesis builder in the middle of a build;
- the anchor (TSA) call;
- the supervisor itself.

2026-10-16: the appraiser is interrupted five ways in a real Body process (SIGKILL before its verdict, mid-verdict and after a whole verdict, the Body's timeout, exit 0 without a readable verdict) and the Body is killed while its appraiser runs; the console and remote channel are SIGKILLed in the inbox write, before and after the rename; the Genesis builder is interrupted in the builder's call, in verification, before the attach and before the deficit is resolved. Still not interrupted anywhere: the anchor (TSA) call and the supervisor itself.

2026-10-16: the declared recovery scenarios (tests/recovery/recovery-tests.yaml) run against real Body processes: SIGKILLs land inside the Gate dispatch of fs.write and brief.engineering (after the claim, before the receipt), before or after the adapter's effect, never partway through it; the deterministic window kills are of a Body.run driver holding the real adapter, restarted as `greg run`; the harness asserts each window and both reconciliation answers were hit.

Proven at BODY_VERIFIED, for kills at the approval boundary, after a torn write, and during read-only mid-dispatch:
- body.recovered is recorded;
- the decision request is remembered, not re-asked (count stays 1);
- the mission resumes after a signed approval, is ACHIEVED, and the separate-process appraisal is VERIFIED;
- verify_chain() passes, and the boot and stop counts are exact;
- the CP-SAT detach survives a SIGKILL;
- the torn tail is quarantined to a sidecar.

At lower tiers: pipeline handoff and self-repair are SANDBOX_VERIFIED (in-process Body, test clock, orderly close and reopen); the goal_chase and local_mission tests are SANDBOX_VERIFIED; the rest are UNIT_VERIFIED.

Not demonstrated: recovery from an interruption between dispatch and receipt of a consequential action through UNCERTAIN, RECONCILIATION, a founder reconcile_* answer, and resumption. No test in the tree reaches greg/missions.py:1076-1088 or :565-576, and the out-of-tree probe shows the post-reconciliation resumption is wrong (see H.3 and H.4). 2026-10-15: that path is now exercised and fixed (gaps G1 and G3: H1 and H3); those regressions are cited under H.3-H.5. 2026-10-16: tests/integration/test_greg_worker_midflight_kill.py (cited here) drives a real Body process through a SIGKILL between the Gate's dispatch claim and its receipt of a worker.commission WorkOrder, a restart that records body.recovered, an UNCERTAIN action bound to the killed grant, exactly one RECONCILIATION, a signed reconcile_not_executed, exactly one re-commission and a closure the separate-process appraisal VERIFIES; its second test kills after the WorkOrder's evidence and before the receipt (gap G11). The restart's UNCERTAIN may come from the write-ahead dispatch recovery (MissionEngine._recover_dispatch) or from the AuthorityOffice's prior-claim guard (greg/authority.py: a claim without a receipt is UNCERTAIN); these tests do not tell the two apart, so they show the recovery path as a whole, not _recover_dispatch specifically (tests/unit/test_greg_recover_dispatch_boundary.py pins that boundary in-process). Stays partial: runtime services other than the Body host are never interrupted (gap G7), and the real-provider worker interruption is operator-run only (H.1a).

2026-10-16: after a restart the closure is appraised again and gets one real VERIFIED verdict; a Body killed mid-build reuses its open deficit and finishes resolving an auto-attach it died inside, and the mission closes VERIFIED.

2026-10-16: across the seeded kills every interrupted dispatch goes through UNCERTAIN, one RECONCILIATION, a signed reconcile_* answer and resumption; a rolled-back or tampered ledger head is refused at boot with nothing written. The rollback guard reads the last heartbeat, so it has a one-tick window, and its tests pin a RUNNING or STOPPED heartbeat and a truncated ledger. After a reconcile_executed answer the separate appraiser REFUTES the closure (exactly-once cannot be re-derived from receipts there); the harness counts that trial as recovered with the verdict withheld.

gaps.next:
Add tests/unit/test_greg_mission_reconciliation.py using an in-process Body (make_body, signed test key, Clock). Register a non-retry_safe internal_write capability costing $0.40 under a $1.00 mission budget; its adapter appends a line to an effect log and then raises RuntimeError.

Assert:
- tick 1 gives UNCERTAIN and exactly 1 RECONCILIATION request, then BLOCKED and WAITING for 5 more ticks with 1 adapter call;
- after a signed reconcile_not_executed, exactly 1 redispatch (attempt 1);
- a second UNCERTAIN opens a NEW RECONCILIATION request with a distinct request_id bound to attempt 1, and the adapter call count stays at 2 for 10 more ticks;
- the reserved exposure (DONE spend plus the cap for each unreconciled UNCERTAIN) never exceeds budget_usd;
- the reconcile_executed path records DONE with 0 further dispatches.

Fix to pair with it: bind the reconciliation scope to the attempt or the uncertain grant_id, and reserve the signed cap for each UNCERTAIN attempt until it is reconciled.

Add the same scenario at BODY tier (see G3). The test fails today, as the out-of-tree probe (scratchpad/probe_h/probe_budget.py) already shows.

Add a unit test with an in-process Body and a read_only retry_safe capability that always raises RuntimeError. Run 20 ticks and assert:
- at most K dispatches (K=3) for that scope;
- after that, a mission.action is recorded as failed, the mission replans, and exactly 1 NO_STRATEGY founder request is raised;
- the count does not reset when the Body is closed and reopened.

Pair it with a MissionState counter of consecutive UNCERTAIN outcomes per scope, rebuilt from the ledger. It fails today (probe.py: 12 of 12 ticks RETRYING).

Add tests/integration/test_greg_body_midflight_kill.py, modelled on test_real_abrupt_process_exit_at_transition. A subprocess driver script opens Body(home), registers a test internal_write capability, and calls Body.run(); the adapter appends to an append-only effect log and then sleeps. The test watches the ledger read-only and sends SIGKILL once a grant_dispatch with no receipt exists for that proposal. It then restarts the same driver under supervisord, using service.supervisord_program with a wrapper command.

Assert:
- body.recovered is recorded;
- mission.action status is UNCERTAIN;
- exactly 1 RECONCILIATION decision.requested exists, and there are 0 further adapter invocations over 10 ticks;
- after a signed `greg decide <rid> reconcile_executed`, the mission is ACHIEVED, the effect log has exactly 1 line, the appraisal is VERIFIED, and verify_chain() passes.

Parametrize the kill to land before dispatch, between dispatch and receipt, and after the receipt but before mission.action.

Use a driver script that registers a ScriptedWorker double in workers.PROVIDERS; it writes a file in the private clone, then sleeps 30s. Run the Body as a separate process, wait for the WorkerLease and the worker PID, and SIGKILL the Body.

Assert:
- the worker PID is gone within 5s (PDEATHSIG under the real Body);
- after restart: UNCERTAIN, then 1 RECONCILIATION request;
- after a signed reconcile_not_executed: the partial clone is discarded, exactly 1 re-commission happens, and appraise gives ACCEPTED;
- booked spend is no more than 2x the signed cap and is never 0 for the interrupted run.

The real-provider variant is blocked: it needs Claude Code CLI credentials and network, so it is an operator-run proof, not CI.

In tests/unit/test_greg_local_mission.py, monkeypatch egregore.local_mission._worker before fork so the worker always calls os._exit(75). Assert:
- worker_exits == [75, 75, 75];
- status BLOCKED, with next_reconsideration naming explicit operator reconciliation;
- grant_dispatch count is at most 1 and receipt count is 0 or 1, with no new grants minted;
- a second supervise() call returns the same blocker without a further fork.

requirements.notes:
BODY_VERIFIED, partial. By clause, on a real `greg run` process (tests/integration/test_whole_machine_trace_process.py), read through the founder's own CLI:
- decisions: the trace shows the ask, then exactly one decision.answered approve with its latency, before the approved write (ordered by ledger record time; before 2026-10-15 the CLI mixed tick time and record time and showed the write before the approve);
- actions: every booked action appears in the trace with the event id and cost the ledger booked (receipts are checked through `greg foundry why` and the approved write's span);
- outcomes: the appraisal spans equal the retained appraisals, the last VERIFIED, verified_rate 1.0;
- why a metric moved: `greg foundry why` on the verified appraisal names the founder's approve as Authority and the receipts of the actions before it; `greg foundry changed blocks` attributes the drop in blocks to the fs.write the founder approved (before 2026-10-15 it blamed whichever capability acted last).
Partial: every cost in this mission is $0 and the span cost is the same ledger field it is compared with, so cost tracing is not independently shown at BODY tier; a nonzero cost through the trace is shown only by tests/unit/test_foundry_systems.py::test_contract_is_audited_and_every_claim_reproduces, which re-derives the system-44 evidence (sample events with nonzero cost); it is not among L1's cited tests.
